.TH XPAR 1 "2026" "xpar 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xpar \- error and erasure correction for guarding data integrity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xpar
.I verb
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B xpar
.I set
.RS
same as
.B xpar verify
.IR set .
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xpar
creates recovery volumes for files and directories. These volumes can restore
data damaged by bad sectors, bit flips, truncation, media rot, or transmission
failures.
.SH VERBS
Any unambiguous prefix of a verb is accepted;  an exact match always wins
over a prefix match.
.TP
.B create
Build a redundancy set from the input paths.
.TP
.B verify
Checks the integrity of the data. Nothing is written to disk. The exit codes
distinguish clean, damaged but repairable, and hopelessly damaged data.
.TP
.B repair
Verify and repair errors or erasures.
.TP
.B scrub
Read all data and recovery volumes and report corrected symbols per codeword.
With
.B \-\-deep
recompute recovery data from the protected data.
.TP
.B extract
Reconstruct data from a split or armoured set (see
.BR \-\-layout ).
A sidecar set protects files already on disk and needs no extraction.
.TP
.B recover
Regenerate one volume of a set from the others.
.TP
.B addrecovery
Add recovery slices to an existing generation.
.TP
.B add
Append a generation protecting new or changed files.
.TP
.B consolidate
Collapse a chain of generations into one through re-encoding.
.TP
.B prune
Remove older generations from a chain, keeping any generation that a
surviving entry still depends on unless
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.B list
Print the manifest of the set.
.TP
.B info
Print geometry, codec, plan and redundancy.
.B \-\-deps
adds the per-generation dependency table that
.B prune
consults.
.TP
.B explain
Print the hand-recovery recipe: the exact
.BR dd (1)
commands that extract the protected data from this particular set without
xpar (without error or erasure correction).
.TP
.B undo
Replay an in-place repair journal after a crash interrupted an operation.
.TP
.B recover\-prologue
Recover the parameters of a destroyed armoured prologue via brute-force.
.TP
.B benchmark
Measure xpar's low-level SIMD kernels.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Repeatable.
.B \-vv
reports per-slice detail.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Diagnostics only.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite, and permit the guarded operations.
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-jobs= N
Worker threads. Default is one per core.
.TP
.BI \-m " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-memory= SIZE
Working-set limit for the planner. When no plan fits, xpar refuses and
names the three numbers that would make one fit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write JSON Lines to the standard output; the human-readable report goes to
the standard error instead.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress ", " \-\-no\-progress
Report progress on the standard error, at most one line a second, or never
report it. Without either, progress is shown only on a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= WHEN
.IR auto " (default), " always ", " never .
.TP
.B \-\-reproducible
Omit the times and the host from the set, ensuring reproducibility across
machines of SHA-exact sets.
.TP
.BI \-\-simd= TIER
Force a SIMD tier.
.I auto
is the default. A tier the hardware cannot run is refused rather than
silently downgraded.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth\-key= FILE
Read the authentication key from
.IR FILE .
An empty file is rejected. Authentication does not encrypt the set.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Sizes take
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", " T
(1024-based) or
.BR KB ", " MB ", " GB ", " TB
(1000-based); an unsuffixed number is bytes.
.SH CREATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " BASE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-output= BASE
Base name for output volumes.
.TP
.BI \-r " SPEC" "\fR, \fP" \-\-recovery= SPEC
Recovery count, percentage, size, or multiple; see
.BR REDUNDANCY .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-recovery= N
Minimum derived recovery-slice count.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-recovery= SPEC
Recovery-axis space reserved for later top-ups.
.TP
.BI \-s " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slice\-size= SIZE
Set slice size. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-b " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slices= N
Plan for this many data slices. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI \-\-layout= WHICH
.IR sidecar " (default), " split ", or " armoured .
.TP
.BI \-\-codec= WHICH
.IR auto " (default), " fft ", or " matrix .
.TP
.BI \-\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit outer field."
.TP
.BI \-\-align= WHICH
.IR none " (default), " slice ", or " 1k .
.TP
.BI \-\-slice\-tag= W
.IR none ", 8 (default), or 16 bytes."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour= WHICH
.IR none ", " metadata ", or " all " (default)."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit inner field."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-t= N
Correct up to
.I N
symbols per inner-code codeword.
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-pct= P
Set inner-code overhead, greater than zero and at most 50 percent.
.TP
.BI \-\-burst= SIZE
Choose interleave depth for this burst size. Excludes
.BR \-\-depth .
.TP
.BI \-\-depth= D
Set interleave depth; default 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-volumes= WHICH
.IR ladder " (default), " equal ", or a positive count."
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup= WHICH
.IR none ", " file " (default), or " chunk .
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-chunk= SIZE
Chunk size for chunk deduplication.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-memory= SIZE
Memory ceiling for the deduplication index.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-max\-refs= N
Maximum aliases of one stored range.
.TP
.BI \-\-preserve= LIST
Metadata to record. Tokens are
.BR mtime , atime , ctime , btime , times , mode , setid , attrs , owner ,
.BR xattr , xattr-all , links , all , none .
A bare list replaces the default; prefixes
.B +
and
.B \-
add and remove tokens. The default is
.BR mtime,mode,attrs,links .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recurse
Descend into directories.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= GLOB
Exclude matching paths; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-include= GLOB
Re-include matching paths; repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-symlinks
Store target content rather than the symbolic link.
.TP
.BI \-\-base= DIR
Store names relative to
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B \-\-labels
Write a label file per volume.
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-only
Omit public CRC and whole-file hashes from an authenticated set.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify\-after
Skip the post-write read-back pass.
.TP
.BI \-\-spool "[=DIR]"
Stage a pipe in a temporary file, optionally under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-stdin\-name= PATH
Manifest path for a lone standard-input argument.
.PP
.B add
and
.B consolidate
also accept the create options.
.SH OTHER VERB OPTIONS
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-fast"
Check tags without the algebraic pass.
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-strong"
Hash every entry as well as its slices.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync=" WHICH
.IR off ", " auto " (default), or " always .
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-step=" N
Sample every
.I Nth
candidate offset.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-window=" SIZE
Search this far on either side of an expected offset.
.TP
.B "verify \-\-resync\-exhaustive"
Confirm every misplaced-data candidate.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-scan=" DIR
Also search
.I DIR
for volumes.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-in\-place" ", " "repair \-\-to=DIR"
Rewrite originals, or write a repaired tree under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-backup" ", " "repair \-\-paranoid"
Keep damaged originals, or re-read and re-verify every write.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-keep\-journal" ", " "repair \-\-no\-journal"
Keep the journal after success, or disable it. These exclude each other.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-dry\-run" ", " "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change"
Report only, or exit 1 when anything was repaired.
.TP
.BR "scrub \-\-deep" ", " "scrub \-\-rewrite" ", " "scrub \-\-rebuild\-cells"
Decode all codewords, write corrections, or rebuild per-cell CRCs.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-to=" DIR
Extract below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B "extract \-\-stdout"
Write the set stream to standard output.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-preserve=" LIST
Apply the metadata classes described under create.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-owner\-map=" WHICH
.IR name " (default) or " numeric .
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-require=" LIST
Turn the named metadata degradations into errors.
.TP
.B "extract \-\-mangle"
Generate DOS 8.3 names where required.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-volume=" WHICH
Regenerate the named or numbered volume.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-to=" DIR
Write the recovered volume below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI "addrecovery \-r " SPEC
Add recovery slices within the reserved axis.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-rescan=" WHICH
.IR stat " (default), " hash ", or " none .
.TP
.BR "add \-\-verify\-unchanged" ", " "add \-\-allow\-missing"
Alias for
.BR \-\-rescan=hash ,
or continue past a vanished entry.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-dedup\-scope=" WHICH
.IR generation " (default) or " chain .
.TP
.BR "consolidate \-\-replace" ", " "consolidate \-\-dry\-run"
Remove collapsed generations, or report without writing.
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-before=" G
Remove every generation before
.IR G .
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-generation=" G
Remove generation
.IR G ;
repeatable.
.TP
.B "prune \-\-dry\-run"
Report without modifying the chain.
.TP
.BR "list \-\-links" ", " "list \-\-dedup"
Group link aliases, or show extents and reference counts.
.TP
.B "info \-\-deps"
Show per-generation dependencies used by
.BR prune .
.TP
.B "benchmark \-\-tiers"
List and time every runnable kernel tier after differential tests.
.PP
.B \-\-generation=G
selects by generation number or set-ID prefix where accepted.
.B \-\-chain
processes every generation oldest first where accepted.
.SH LAYOUTS
.TP
.B sidecar
Original files remain external. The index is
.IB base .xpa;
recovery volumes contain packets. Originals have outer-code protection but
are not inner-armoured.
.TP
.B split
Bare
.IB base .d NN
volumes hold consecutive set-stream ranges with no header, trailer, or tail
padding. Concatenating them numerically yields the exact stream. Index and
recovery volumes contain packets.
.TP
.B armoured
One
.IB base .xpa
contains three protected prologue copies and an inner-coded packet stream
holding manifest, data, and recovery. Use
.B extract
rather than concatenation.
.PP
Names are not identities. Readers bind volumes and generations by set IDs,
parent IDs, layout entries, and bare-volume content tags.
.SH REDUNDANCY
.B \-r
accepts four forms:
.TP
.B 100
a slice count.
.TP
.B 15.7%
a percentage of the protected data.
.TP
.B 2.5M
an absolute number of recovery bytes.
.TP
.B 1x
a multiple of the data, so
.B \-r 1x
is mirroring.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.BR verify ,
no damage.
.TP
.B 1
.BR verify :
damage found and repairable.
.BR "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change" :
something was repaired.
.TP
.B 2
Damage found and not repairable with the available recovery.
.TP
.B 3
Set not found, not an xpar set, or an unsupported format version.
.TP
.B 4
Usage error.
.TP
.B 5
I/O error.
.TP
.B 6
Authentication failure: missing key, wrong key, or MAC mismatch.
.TP
.B 7
No feasible plan under
.BR \-m ,
or out of memory.
.TP
.B 8
Internal error. This is a bug.
.PP
.B repair
without
.B \-\-exit\-on\-change
exits 0 whether or not it had to do anything, which is what a cron job
wants.
.B verify
distinguishes 0, 1 and 2, which is what a monitoring check wants.
.SH EXAMPLES
Protect a file at 10% redundancy, then check it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 10% \-o backup movie.mkv
xpar verify backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Repair in place after a bad sector, keeping an undo journal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar repair \-\-in\-place backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Protect a directory tree, deduplicating identical files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 15% \-\-dedup=file \-o photos \-R ~/Pictures
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a medium age. The histogram of corrected symbols per codeword is the
only signal that a disc is degrading before it fails:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar scrub photos.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the recipe that recovers the data without xpar:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar explain backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If present, implies
.BR \-\-reproducible .
The value is not embedded in the set. Unless explicitly requested with
.BR \-\-preserve ,
timestamps and host-dependent ownership metadata are omitted and the creator
string contains no version.
.SH SECURITY
Input volumes and manifests are by default considered untrusted.  xpar validates
packet and nested record lengths before allocation, the total allocation is also
further bounded by the input size, and continues past isolated invalid packets
to find replicated copies.
.PP
Extraction rejects empty and absolute names, drive and UNC names,
.B .
and
.B ..
components, control bytes, duplicate output paths, and invalid path
components (platform-specific). It does not follow an existing symlink
while constructing an output path. Hard-link targets must be regular
entries in the same effective manifest. Privileged mode bits,
ownership, extended attributes and links are applied only when
requested; unsupported restoration is reported, and
.B \-\-require
makes it fatal.
.PP
.B \-\-auth\-key
authenticates metadata and content but provides neither encryption nor
freshness.
An authenticated archive requires the key for every operation. Public hashes
retained without
.B \-\-auth\-only
remain useful after authentication, but their containing packet cannot be
trusted without its packet key.
.SH VERSION 1 ARCHIVES
Version 2.0 does not read version 1 archives and ships no version 1
decoder. A version 1 file handed to any verb is detected and refused with
a message naming the version. To migrate, decode with xpar 1.x and
re-protect with this version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB base .xpa
The index volume: the manifest, the slice tables and the volume layout.
.TP
.IB base .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume holding
.I MM
recovery slices starting at exponent
.IR NN .
Each number is padded to two digits, or to as many as the widest value in
that position needs.
.TP
.IB base .d NN
A bare split-layout data volume. Numeric order reconstructs the set stream.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .xpa
The index volume for generation
.I GGG
after generation zero.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .d NN
A split data volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .d NN .xpa
Optional volume label written beside each bare data volume by
.BR \-\-labels .
.TP
.IB base .xparundo
An in-place repair journal. Its presence means a repair was interrupted;
.B xpar undo
replays it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR par2 (1)
